      modelAnswer: 'A static data structure has its size fixed at compile time (or at the moment of declaration) and that size cannot change while the program is running; a typical example is an array. Memory is allocated in a single contiguous block, which makes access fast (constant-time indexing) but wastes space if the structure is not fully used, and overflows if more elements are needed than were declared. A dynamic data structure can grow and shrink during run time as nodes are added or removed; examples include linked lists, dynamic arrays (ArrayList, vector), trees and queues built on linked nodes. Memory is allocated on the heap as needed, so only the space actually required is used, but there is overhead in pointer/reference fields and operations are typically slower per element because nodes are not contiguous in memory.'
